; The default landscape (GAMEPLAY-DESIGN P1.9): rolling green hills, the profile
; the non-desert maps use.
;
; SMOOTH GROUND OVER A VAST REACH. The world's size is not set here - it comes
; from `TerrainMesh.ring_count`, since concentric detail rings make reach grow
; exponentially while cost grows linearly. What this file sets is the LANDSCAPE:
; how tall the hills are, how far apart, and how sharp.
;
; amplitude_m 90 with noise_frequency 0.004 gives hills roughly 250 m apart and
; 90 m tall. Both were measured rather than guessed - `amplitude_m` means the
; metres it says, because the field is normalised against fractal noise's real
; span rather than its nominal one.
;
; noise_octaves 2 rather than 4, and that was a LOOK decision taken by rendering
; all three: at four octaves the fine detail dominated and the ground became busy
; noise with nowhere flat to land. Two octaves gives hills, valleys and ground.
;
; ridge 0.35 is the character dial. At 0 the terrain rolls like dunes; at 1 it
; grows ridge lines and canyon walls. It is the number to move first if the
; landscape reads as bland, before touching amplitude.
;
; clearing_radius_m 0 by default. A sortie's objective and its garrison rings
; need level ground, and nothing yet flattens a footprint on demand - so the
; arena floor is available as a blunt instrument and simply is not switched on.

[gd_resource type="Resource" script_class="TerrainConfig" load_steps=2 format=3]

[ext_resource type="Script" path="res://resources/terrain_config.gd" id="1_tcfg"]

[resource]
script = ExtResource("1_tcfg")
extent_m = 320.0
cell_m = 4.0
amplitude_m = 90.0
noise_frequency = 0.004
noise_octaves = 2
ridge = 0.35
floor_m = -30.0
clearing_radius_m = 0.0
clearing_falloff_m = 70.0
